{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And HaShem saw that the wickedness of man was great, etc. And HaShem regretted that He had made, etc.</b> One must understand His saying <b>the wickedness of man on the earth</b> — why does He attribute the wickedness to the earth, so that it appears their wickedness was in the body of the earth itself. Yet in truth the wickedness of those wicked men was in licentiousness, which is explicitly stated in the verse, and in violence and robbery. And even though it would be justified to say ״on the earth״ with regard to the aspect of robbery of the earth, even so this would be merely a detail within the whole. Further, why did He precisely say <b>and HaShem regretted [that He had made man] on the earth</b> — why did He say the word ״on the earth״? For it appears that if He had made him in another place He would never have regretted it, and there is no sense in this. Further, how can it be justified to say ״and HaShem regretted,״ for He is not a man, etc. Further, His saying <b>and it grieved Him</b> — why this grief? For after all, He exacts His vengeance from them and obliterates every rebel. And regarding this, one may speak of the loss of the creation." "<b>However,</b> the intention of the verses is this: man is composed of four elements — fire, air (ruach), water, and earth (dust) — and they are in gradations. The lowest of the four is earth, for it is the most turbid and coarse of all. Above it is the element of water; above that, the element of fire; above fire, the element of air — which is the most spiritual of them all. And you will find that all created beings in the world, although all of them were created from four elements, nevertheless each creature's essential structure is from one element, which is the root element, and the other three are secondary to it. There is a creature whose root element is water, such as fish that grow in water. There are those whose root element is air, such as birds; and although they were created from the fluid, nevertheless HaShem strengthened in them the element of air, and therefore birds do not rest — they fly, which corresponds to the nature of air. And there is a creature whose root is fire, such as the salamander. But man — the element upon which he is built is the element of earth, as it is written (Genesis 2:7): ״וַיִּיצֶר ה׳ etc. et ha-adam afar min ha-adamah״ — which is the coarsest and thickest of the four elements. And therefore his dwelling and rest are on the earth, and he cannot tolerate living in water, or in air, or in fire. And HaShem did so for many reasons, one of which is that man is the ladder by which the degrees ascend from below to above, and the lowest degree is the element of earth, and it must be on the earth. And if his foundation had been built from the other three, which are spiritual, in equal measure to earth, the intention of his creation would not have been complete. And the discerning one will understand, for this is the sod of the berur (refinement/sifting). Understand this." "And therefore, when HaShem saw that the wickedness of man was great, He recognized that the preponderance of repugnance was due to his element — for he is built from the earth, and his place of rest and vitality is on the earth. This is what is meant by <b>and HaShem saw that the wickedness of man was great on the earth</b> — for it is the coarsest element and the utter opposite of the spiritual. And therefore HaShem regretted that He had made man on the earth and had not made him built from the remaining finer elements — for had He done so, there would have been evil all day long. But since making him from the other elements would not have fulfilled the intention of creation that HaShem had conceived in His heart to enact, this is what is meant by <b>and it grieved Him to His heart</b> — and it does not say בְּלִבּוֹ (״in His heart״), but אֶל לִבּוֹ (״to His heart״): meaning, to His will and the thought He had conceived in creating man. And from this perspective HaShem did not overturn the creation — for otherwise HaShem would have demolished and rebuilt with a new structure composed of three elements. And therefore, although He brought the Flood and destroyed everything, He left the creation as it had been, for the reason concealed in His heart, blessed be He. And although with the Master, blessed be He, it is not fitting to say ״and He regretted,״ for He looks and foresees to the end of all generations, He said so because the Master's, blessed be He, attributes are attributes of mercy, yet there is also the aspect of din (judgment) in Him, blessed be He. And when the creatures walk in a straight path, YHWH, He is Elohim — the attribute of din is joined to the attribute of mercy. But when the wicked provoke, the attribute of mercy withdraws its compassion and chooses the negation of existence, to execute judgment upon them. And this is what is meant by <b>and HaShem regretted</b> — meaning: the aspect of mercy prefers that the creation not be, so as not to overturn the attribute; yet for this reason the creation is not annulled." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that one may say according to what the Rambam wrote in chapter 5 of Hilkhot Teshuvah, and this is his language: ״For His name, may it be exalted, He and His knowledge are one, and the mind of man cannot grasp this matter fully,״ etc. — ״and we have no power to know how the Holy One, blessed be He, knows the creatures and their deeds, but we know for certain that the deeds of man are in man's hand, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not pull him,״ etc., end of quote. And the Raavad, may HaShem atone for him, conducted himself with slight disrespect toward the Rambam regarding his words on this matter, and proposed that it would be better to say that His vision, blessed be He, is like the vision of an astrologer, etc. — one should examine his words there. But the words of the Rambam are the principle, for the manner of HaShem's knowledge is not graspable by us, and who would compare to HaShem to take knowledge from what is likened to Him. And I will give you insight (ve-aschilkha): HaShem is able to negate the knowledge that is graspable by His knowledge, so that He does not know it — when the Master wills it — which is something man has no power to do, namely that knowledge reaches him yet he does not know it. And this is what the Rambam hinted at in saying: ״and the mind of man cannot grasp,״ etc. — for how is it possible that the knowledge reaches Him by itself yet is negated by itself? And this is what the verse says (Numbers 23:21): ״לֹא הִבִּיט אָוֶן בְּיַעֲקֹב וְלֹא רָאָה עָמָל״. You see that when HaShem does not wish to know, the knowledge is negated even though the knowledge exists of itself — for He is not in the category of man, for whom knowledge is outside himself, to say that if he withholds attention he does not know. Not so; and even so, as it were, there is this aspect, as it is written ״לֹא הִבִּיט״ etc." "From this we can now say that HaShem, at the time of creating man, negated from His knowledge the transgressions of man, for two reasons: first, for the simple reason that HaShem is good and does not look upon repugnance. But this reason alone would not suffice, since He needs knowledge to know what to do and what follows. Rather, there is a second reason, which is that it was wise of HaShem to do so, so that the claim of the wicked not arise — that they would say: His knowledge is coercive. For since HaShem knew that this person would transgress what he transgressed, the matter is forced to be so by itself, even though HaShem did not decree it to be so. And from here on there is no punishment for the wicked, for from the wicked no wickedness proceeds — and with what would a wicked man be deterred and cease from wrongdoing? Therefore HaShem removed this knowledge of the deeds of the wicked. But the deeds of the righteous He did not remove from His knowledge, and in them He rejoiced. And for this reason the creation came into being, as the verse says (Genesis 1:31): ״וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים אֶת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה וְהִנֵּה טוֹב מְאֹד״. And if you would say: if He did not look upon the iniquity of the wicked, how could He fix a punishment for doers of wickedness? — but does one need to fix a punishment for this particular person who acts wickedly? One need not fix a punishment except for the doer of iniquity and transgression. And regarding the righteous, there is no room for the claim that His knowledge is coercive, for this claim is a demand for payment, and HaShem desires to pay and there is no claimant against Himself. But regarding the punishment of the wicked, they will come with a claim so as not to accept punishment; therefore HaShem safeguarded the matter. And one cannot argue and say: if HaShem had looked at them when they would be righteous, they would have been doers of righteousness — for this claim is no claim to exempt them from the particulars of free choice that He placed in their hands, and they chose evil and rejected good of their own will. And after Elohim has informed us of this, there is no wonder in His saying ״וַיִּנָּחֶם ה׳״ etc., ״כִּי נִחַמְתִּי״ — for He Himself negated this knowledge from Himself."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:59.510196212Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}